from data_new import X,t,CW,SR
X("amphetamine","Amphetamine (Adderall, Dexedrine, Evekeo)","stim","Releases dopamine and noradrenaline; central nervous system stimulant.","Adderall and Adderall XR (mixed amphetamine salts), Mydayis, Dexedrine and Zenzedi (dextroamphetamine), Evekeo, Adzenys. Street: addies, speed, uppers.",
"First synthesized by Lazăr Edeleanu in 1887. Gordon Alles described its effects in 1929, and Smith, Kline & French sold the Benzedrine inhaler from 1933. Soldiers in World War II were given amphetamines. Adderall, originally the diet drug Obetrol, was approved for ADHD in 1996.",
"Historically for nasal congestion, depression, narcolepsy and weight loss. Today: ADHD and narcolepsy.","Students and workers misuse it for focus and staying awake; it is also used for euphoria. Counterfeit 'Adderall' made with methamphetamine is common.","US Schedule II; UN 1971 Convention Schedule II.",
["Amphetamine's character is driven focus: energy, confidence, talkativeness and narrowed attention, with less hunger and need for sleep.","At higher levels, euphoria grows along with a racing heart, jaw clenching, sweating and anxiety; people may stay awake for long periods.","The comedown brings irritability, low mood, fatigue and hunger. Repeated high use can cause paranoia, psychosis and heart damage."],
["Swallowed immediate-release amphetamine starts in about 30 to 60 minutes: alertness and a lift in mood.","Focus and energy build; appetite fades.","The peak, around 2 to 3 hours, brings the most energy and the highest heart rate.","IR effects last about 4 to 6 hours; XR products about 10 to 12.","Comedown brings tiredness and irritability.","Trouble sleeping, low appetite.","Next-day fatigue and low mood after heavy use."],
{"oral":t("30-60 min","1-2 h","2-3 h","3-6 h, XR 8-12 h","6-12 h","12-24 h","next day")},
{"oral":"Tablets and capsules."},
"Amphetamine overdose causes chest pain, high blood pressure, overheating, seizures, stroke and psychosis.",
"Per DailyMed, brand Adderall tablets are blue (lowest strength) or orange (higher strengths), round or oval, scored, and debossed 'dp' with the strength number. Adderall XR capsules have blue or orange bodies. Counterfeit Adderall made with methamphetamine has caused deaths. "+SR,
"Amphetamine is processed partly by CYP2D6 and excreted in urine, faster when urine is acidic.",
er="Immediate-release amphetamine (Adderall, Evekeo, Zenzedi) works for about 4 to 6 hours. Extended-release forms (Adderall XR beads, Mydayis, Adzenys XR-ODT, Dyanavel XR) release drug in stages over about 10 to 16 hours, with a smoother rise and fall. They are designed to be taken exactly as directed; damaging them releases more drug at once and is dangerous.",
skip="Snorting or injecting crushed tablets is misuse with added dangers and is not covered.",
src=["Rasmussen N. America's first amphetamine epidemic 1929-1971. Am J Public Health 2008","DailyMed: Adderall and Adderall XR labels"])

X("lisdexamfetamine","Lisdexamfetamine (Vyvanse)","stim","Prodrug converted to dextroamphetamine in the blood.","Vyvanse (capsules and chewables).",
"Developed by New River Pharmaceuticals and approved in 2007 for ADHD, and in 2015 for binge-eating disorder.","ADHD and moderate-to-severe binge-eating disorder.","Less misused than IR amphetamine because the prodrug design slows onset by any route, but still misused for focus.","US Schedule II.",
["Vyvanse feels smoother and longer than Adderall: a gradual rise into steady focus and energy, with a gentler comedown.","Side effects include low appetite, insomnia, dry mouth, anxiety and raised heart rate.","Because red blood cells must convert it, other routes do not make it faster, but higher amounts still cause stimulant toxicity."],
["Effects start about 1 to 2 hours after swallowing.","A gradual lift in focus.","Peak around 3 to 4 hours.","Steady effects for 10 to 14 hours.","Gentle comedown in the evening.","Insomnia if taken late.","Next-day fatigue after heavy use."],
{"oral":t("1-2 h","2-3 h","3-4 h","10-14 h","12-14 h","14-24 h","next day")},{"oral":"Capsules and chewable tablets."},"Overdose causes the same emergencies as amphetamine, delayed by several hours.",
"Per DailyMed, Vyvanse capsules have colored caps and bodies printed 'S489' with the strength; chewables are round and imprinted 'S489'.","Lisdexamfetamine is converted to dextroamphetamine by enzymes in red blood cells.",
er="Vyvanse is long-acting by its chemistry (a prodrug), not by a coating, so it releases steadily however it is taken; higher amounts still cause harm.",src=["DailyMed: Vyvanse label"])

X("methylphenidate","Methylphenidate (Ritalin, Concerta)","stim","Dopamine and noradrenaline reuptake inhibitor.","Ritalin, Ritalin LA, Concerta, Metadate CD, Methylin, Daytrana patch, Jornay PM, QuilliChew; dexmethylphenidate: Focalin. Street: kiddie coke, vitamin R.",
"Synthesized by Leandro Panizzon at Ciba in 1944 and named Ritalin after his wife Marguerite, nicknamed Rita; approved in the US in 1955. It became the leading ADHD medicine in the 1990s.",
"ADHD and narcolepsy.","Misused for studying; injecting tablets causes lung damage from fillers.","US Schedule II; UN 1971 Convention Schedule II.",
["Methylphenidate feels more 'clean' and shorter than amphetamine: alertness and focus, but often more jittery and with a steeper comedown.","Side effects include low appetite, headache, stomach pain, insomnia and raised heart rate.","Misuse can cause anxiety, paranoia and dangerous heart effects."],
["IR Ritalin starts within 20 to 60 minutes.","Alertness rises.","Peak about 1 to 2 hours.","IR lasts about 3 to 4 hours; Concerta about 10 to 12.","Comedown brings irritability ('rebound').","Insomnia and low appetite.","Mild next-day fatigue."],
{"oral":t("20-60 min","about 1 h","1-2 h","3-4 h, ER up to 12 h","4-12 h","12-24 h","next day"),"transdermal":t("about 2 h","2-4 h","several hours","while worn plus a few hours","after removal","hours","next day")},
{"oral":"Tablets, capsules, chewables, liquids.","transdermal":"Daytrana patch."},"Overdose causes agitation, tremor, high fever, fast heart rhythms, seizures and psychosis.",
"Per DailyMed, Concerta tablets are capsule-shaped and printed 'alza' with the strength number; IR Ritalin tablets are small and round. "+SR,
"Methylphenidate is broken down by carboxylesterase 1 (CES1); alcohol forms ethylphenidate and raises levels.",
er="Immediate-release methylphenidate lasts about 3 to 4 hours. Extended-release forms include Concerta (an osmotic tablet whose empty shell may pass in stool), Ritalin LA and Metadate CD (bead capsules), Daytrana (patch) and Jornay PM (taken in the evening). These last about 8 to 12 hours. Damaging them is dangerous.",
src=["Morton WA, Stockton GG. Methylphenidate abuse and psychiatric side effects. Prim Care Companion J Clin Psychiatry 2000","DailyMed: Concerta and Ritalin labels"])

X("methamphetamine","Methamphetamine","stim","Potent dopamine releaser.","Desoxyn (prescription); street: meth, crystal, ice, glass, tina, crank.",
"First made from ephedrine by Nagai Nagayoshi in 1893 and crystallized by Akira Ogata in 1919. It was given to soldiers in World War II (Pervitin in Germany, Philopon in Japan). Illicit meth spread in the US from the 1960s; today most comes from large-scale cartel labs and is very pure.",
"Desoxyn is approved for ADHD and formerly for obesity, but is rarely prescribed.","Smoked, snorted, swallowed or injected for euphoria and energy; a major cause of overdose deaths, often with fentanyl.","US Schedule II; UN 1971 Convention Schedule II.",
["Methamphetamine's character is a powerful, long rush of euphoria, confidence, energy and sexual drive lasting many hours, far longer than cocaine.","People stay awake for days, stop eating and develop repetitive behaviors; paranoia and hallucinations, such as feeling bugs under the skin, are common with heavy use.","Long-term use damages teeth ('meth mouth'), heart, skin and brain dopamine systems."],
["Smoked, the rush comes within seconds; swallowed, within 20 to 30 minutes.","Intense euphoria and energy.","The peak lasts much longer than cocaine.","Effects last 8 to 12 hours or more.","The crash brings exhaustion and depression.","Long sleep and extreme hunger.","Days of low mood and craving."],
{"inhaled-smoked":t("seconds","minutes","15-60 min","4-8 h","8-12 h","1-3 days","days"),"oral":t("20-30 min","1-2 h","2-4 h","6-10 h","10-14 h","1-3 days","days"),"intranasal":t("3-5 min","10-30 min","30-60 min","4-8 h","8-12 h","1-3 days","days"),"injected":t("seconds","minutes","15-30 min","4-8 h","8-12 h","1-3 days","days")},
{"inhaled-smoked":"Smoked.","oral":"Swallowed.","intranasal":"Snorted.","injected":"Injected."},"Meth overdose causes overheating, heart attack, stroke, seizures and psychosis; fentanyl contamination adds breathing failure.",
"Crystal meth looks like clear or white glassy shards; it also appears pressed into counterfeit Adderall or 'ecstasy' pills. "+SR,
"Methamphetamine has a long half-life (around 10 hours) and is processed partly by CYP2D6.",src=["NIDA: Methamphetamine Research Report","Rasmussen N. On Speed: The Many Lives of Amphetamine (2008)"])

X("cocaine","Cocaine","stim","Dopamine, noradrenaline and serotonin reuptake inhibitor; also a local anesthetic.","Medical: cocaine hydrochloride nasal solution (Goprelto, Numbrino). Street: coke, blow, snow, yayo.",
"Coca leaf has been chewed in the Andes for thousands of years. Albert Niemann isolated cocaine in 1860; Carl Koller used it as an eye anesthetic in 1884, and Freud promoted it. It was in Coca-Cola until about 1903 and restricted by the Harrison Act of 1914.",
"Still used as a topical anesthetic that also shrinks blood vessels in nose and throat procedures.","Popular since the 1970s and 1980s; today many cocaine-involved deaths also involve fentanyl.","US Schedule II; UN 1961 Single Convention.",
["Cocaine's character is a short, bright burst of confidence, talkativeness and euphoria, followed quickly by craving for more.","The heart races, blood vessels tighten and body temperature rises; even young, healthy people can have heart attacks or strokes.","With alcohol, the liver makes cocaethylene, which is more toxic to the heart. Repeated use brings anxiety, paranoia and nasal damage."],
["Snorted, effects begin within a few minutes: numbness, alertness and euphoria.","Confidence and energy surge.","The peak comes within 15 to 30 minutes.","Effects last about 30 to 60 minutes.","The comedown brings anxiety and craving.","Restlessness and low mood.","Next-day fatigue and depression after binges."],
{"intranasal":t("1-5 min","5-15 min","15-30 min","30-60 min","1-2 h","2-6 h","next day"),"injected":t("seconds","about 1 min","5-10 min","15-30 min","30-60 min","hours","next day")},
{"intranasal":"Snorted powder.","injected":"Injected."},"Cocaine overdose causes chest pain, heart attack, stroke, seizures and overheating; fentanyl contamination is now a leading cause of death.",
"Powder cocaine is white and crystalline and is often cut with other substances, including levamisole (which can destroy white blood cells) and fentanyl."+CW,
"Cocaine is broken down quickly by blood and liver esterases, which is why it is short-acting.",src=["NIDA: Cocaine Research Report"])

X("crack-cocaine","Crack cocaine","stim","Smokable base form of cocaine.","Crack, rock, hard, base.",
"Crack spread in US cities in the mid-1980s; the 1986 Anti-Drug Abuse Act set far harsher penalties than for powder, a disparity reduced by the Fair Sentencing Act of 2010.","None.","Cheap and intensely reinforcing; associated with the 1980s crack epidemic.","Same as cocaine (US Schedule II).",
["Crack produces an immediate, intense rush within seconds that fades within minutes, driving rapid, repeated use.","Craving is intense, and binges can last until the drug or money runs out.","Smoking causes lung injury ('crack lung'), burns and cracked lips; shared pipes can spread hepatitis C."],
["Effects begin within seconds.","An intense rush.","Peak within minutes.","Effects last about 5 to 15 minutes.","A sharp crash and craving.","Exhaustion after binges.","Days of low mood and craving."],
{"inhaled-smoked":t("seconds","seconds","1-5 min","5-15 min","15-30 min","hours","days")},{"inhaled-smoked":"Smoked."},"Overdose risks are the same as cocaine, made more likely by binging; fentanyl contamination is common.",
"Crack looks like off-white rocks or chunks."+CW,"Smoking delivers cocaine to the brain within seconds.",src=["NIDA: Cocaine Research Report","US Sentencing Commission reports on cocaine sentencing"])

X("modafinil","Modafinil and armodafinil (Provigil, Nuvigil)","stim","Wakefulness-promoting agent; weak dopamine reuptake inhibitor.","Provigil, Nuvigil; Modalert and Waklert (India); street: moda.",
"Developed in France by Lafon Laboratories in the 1970s and 1980s and approved in the US in 1998; armodafinil (the R-enantiomer) in 2007.","Narcolepsy, sleepiness from obstructive sleep apnea, and shift-work disorder.","Used as a 'smart drug' by students and workers, often bought online without a prescription.","US Schedule IV.",
["Modafinil feels like steady wakefulness rather than a rush: clear and alert, sometimes flat, with little euphoria.","Side effects include headache, nausea, anxiety and insomnia.","Rare but serious skin reactions (Stevens-Johnson syndrome) can occur, and it makes hormonal birth control less effective."],
["Effects begin about 1 hour after swallowing.","Alertness builds.","Peak around 2 to 4 hours.","Wakefulness lasts 10 to 15 hours.","Gradual fade.","Insomnia if taken late.","Usually little next-day effect."],
{"oral":t("about 1 h","1-2 h","2-4 h","10-15 h","12-16 h","next night","next day")},{"oral":"Tablets."},"Overdose causes agitation, insomnia, fast heart rate and high blood pressure; deaths are rare.",
"Per DailyMed, Provigil tablets are white and capsule-shaped, debossed 'PROVIGIL' with the strength number. Products bought online are often unregulated.","Metabolized by CYP3A4; it induces enzymes that reduce birth control effectiveness.",src=["DailyMed: Provigil and Nuvigil labels"])

X("caffeine","Caffeine","stim","Adenosine receptor blocker.","Coffee, tea, energy drinks, NoDoz, Vivarin; pure caffeine powders; caffeine citrate (for premature babies).",
"Tea has been drunk in China for thousands of years, and coffee spread from Ethiopia and Yemen in the 1400s. Friedlieb Runge isolated caffeine in 1819.","Apnea of prematurity; added to some headache medicines.","The most widely used psychoactive drug in the world.","Legal; in 2018 the FDA declared bulk pure and highly concentrated caffeine products sold to consumers unlawful because of deaths.",
["Caffeine brings alertness, reduced fatigue and a mild mood lift by blocking adenosine, a chemical that builds sleep pressure.","Too much causes jitters, anxiety, fast heartbeat, stomach upset and insomnia.","Pure caffeine powders and concentrated liquids have caused deaths, because a tiny amount can be lethal."],
["Effects begin within 15 to 45 minutes.","Alertness rises.","Peak at about 1 hour.","Effects last 3 to 5 hours.","Gradual fade.","Possible headache.","Regular users get withdrawal headaches if they skip a day."],
{"oral":t("15-45 min","30-60 min","about 1 h","3-5 h","5-8 h","8-12 h","next day (withdrawal headache)")},{"oral":"Drinks and tablets."},"Caffeine overdose causes vomiting, fast irregular heartbeat and seizures, and can be fatal with concentrated products.",
"Caffeine pills are usually white tablets; pure powders are fine and white.","Caffeine is processed by CYP1A2; smokers clear it faster, while pregnancy and some medicines slow it.",src=["FDA: Pure and highly concentrated caffeine guidance (2018)"])

X("nicotine","Nicotine","stim","Nicotinic acetylcholine receptor agonist.","Cigarettes, cigars, vapes, nicotine pouches (such as Zyn), chewing tobacco; medicines: patches, gum, lozenges, inhaler, nasal spray.",
"Tobacco was used in the Americas for centuries and brought to Europe in the 1500s. Nicotine was isolated in 1828. The 1964 US Surgeon General's report linked smoking to lung cancer.","Nicotine replacement therapy helps people quit smoking.","Smoking remains the leading preventable cause of death; vaping and pouches are common among young people.","Legal for adults 21 and over in the US (Tobacco 21, 2019).",
["Nicotine gives a quick lift of alertness and calm at the same time, which is part of why it is so habit-forming.","Strong dependence develops; withdrawal brings irritability, anxiety and craving.","Liquid nicotine is highly poisonous to children if swallowed. Most harm from smoking comes from the smoke itself, not the nicotine."],
["Inhaled effects begin within seconds.","A brief buzz.","Peak within minutes.","Effects last 30 minutes to an hour.","Craving returns as levels fall.","Irritability.","Withdrawal without use."],
{"inhaled-smoked":t("seconds","seconds","1-5 min","15-60 min","1-2 h","hours","withdrawal"),"oral":t("minutes","10-20 min","20-40 min","1-2 h","about 2 h","hours","withdrawal"),"transdermal":t("1-2 h","2-4 h","several hours","while worn","hours after removal","hours","next day")},
{"inhaled-smoked":"Smoked or vaped.","oral":"Gum, lozenges and pouches.","transdermal":"Patches."},"Nicotine poisoning causes vomiting, sweating, fast heart rate and seizures; a child who swallows e-liquid needs emergency care.",
"Vapes are small devices; pouches are small white packets. Keep e-liquids and pouches away from children.","Nicotine is cleared by CYP2A6; slower metabolizers tend to smoke less.",src=["CDC: Health effects of cigarette smoking","US Surgeon General's reports on smoking"])

X("phentermine","Phentermine and older appetite suppressants","stim","Amphetamine-like appetite suppressants (anorectics).","Phentermine (Adipex-P, Lomaira; with topiramate in Qsymia), diethylpropion (Tenuate), phendimetrazine (Bontril), benzphetamine (Didrex); withdrawn: fenfluramine (Pondimin), sibutramine (Meridia).",
"Phentermine was approved in 1959. In the 1990s 'fen-phen' (fenfluramine with phentermine) was linked to heart valve damage and pulmonary hypertension, and fenfluramine was withdrawn in 1997. Sibutramine was withdrawn in 2010 for heart risks.",
"Short-term weight loss; fenfluramine returned in 2020 (Fintepla) for rare childhood epilepsies.","Misused for energy and weight loss; less euphoric than amphetamine.","Phentermine and diethylpropion: Schedule IV; phendimetrazine and benzphetamine: Schedule III.",
["Phentermine feels like a mild amphetamine: alert, wired and not hungry, with dry mouth and insomnia.","It raises heart rate and blood pressure and can cause anxiety.","Older anorectics carried risks of heart valve disease and pulmonary hypertension."],
["Effects begin within about 1 hour.","Energy rises and appetite drops.","Peak around 3 to 4 hours.","Effects last much of the day.","Comedown brings fatigue.","Insomnia.","Possible next-day fatigue."],
{"oral":t("about 1 h","1-3 h","3-4 h","8-12 h","12-16 h","next night","next day")},{"oral":"Capsules and tablets."},"Overdose causes agitation, high blood pressure, fast heart rate and psychosis.",
"Per DailyMed, Adipex-P capsules are blue and white, and Adipex-P tablets are white with blue specks. "+SR,"Phentermine releases noradrenaline.",src=["Connolly HM. et al. Valvular heart disease associated with fenfluramine-phentermine. NEJM 1997","DailyMed: Adipex-P label"])

X("ephedrine","Ephedrine, pseudoephedrine and ephedra","stim","Plant-derived and synthetic stimulants that release noradrenaline.","Ephedra (ma huang), Primatene tablets, Bronkaid; pseudoephedrine: Sudafed.",
"Ma huang has been used in Chinese medicine for thousands of years. Nagai Nagayoshi isolated ephedrine in 1885. The FDA banned ephedra supplements in 2004 after deaths, and the Combat Methamphetamine Epidemic Act of 2005 moved pseudoephedrine behind the pharmacy counter.",
"Low blood pressure during anesthesia, asthma (limited), and nasal congestion (pseudoephedrine).","Used in weight-loss and bodybuilding products, often with caffeine.","Ephedrine and pseudoephedrine are DEA List I chemicals with sales limits.",
["Ephedrine feels like jittery, physical stimulation more than euphoria: racing heart, sweating and alertness.","It raises blood pressure and can trigger heart attacks, strokes and seizures, especially with caffeine.","Pseudoephedrine is milder but causes insomnia and raised blood pressure."],
["Effects start within 30 to 60 minutes.","Energy and jitteriness.","Peak at 1 to 2 hours.","Effects last 3 to 6 hours.","Fade with tiredness.","Insomnia.","Usually none."],
{"oral":t("30-60 min","about 1 h","1-2 h","3-6 h","6-8 h","8-12 h","next day")},{"oral":"Tablets."},"High levels cause dangerous blood pressure spikes, heart rhythm problems and strokes.",
"Sudafed tablets are small and red; generics vary.","Ephedrine releases noradrenaline and is cleared mostly unchanged by the kidneys.",src=["FDA: Final rule declaring ephedra supplements adulterated (2004)","DEA Diversion: Combat Methamphetamine Epidemic Act"])

# ---- cathinones
X("khat-cathinone","Khat and cathinone","cath","Natural stimulants from the khat plant.","Khat, qat, chat, miraa.",
"Khat leaves have been chewed in the Horn of Africa and Yemen for centuries. Cathinone was identified as the main stimulant in 1975.","None.","Chewed socially in East African and Yemeni communities.","Cathinone is US Schedule I and cathine Schedule IV; under the UN 1971 Convention they are Schedules I and III. The UK banned khat in 2014.",
["Chewing khat brings mild stimulation, talkativeness and euphoria over hours, far milder than synthetic cathinones.","It raises heart rate and blood pressure and suppresses appetite and sleep.","Long-term heavy use is linked to mouth disease, liver injury and psychosis."],
["Effects begin within 15 to 30 minutes of chewing.","Gradual alertness.","Peak over 1 to 2 hours.","Effects last several hours.","Comedown brings low mood and insomnia.","Fatigue.","Next-day lethargy."],
{"oral":t("15-30 min","about 1 h","1-2 h","3-4 h","4-6 h","hours","next day")},{"oral":"Fresh leaves chewed."},"Serious overdose is rare; heavy use harms the heart and liver.","Fresh green leaves; cathinone breaks down within days.","Cathinone is the natural beta-keto relative of amphetamine.",src=["WHO Expert Committee on Drug Dependence: khat assessment (2006)"])

X("mephedrone","Mephedrone (4-MMC)","cath","Synthetic cathinone releasing dopamine and serotonin.","Meow meow, M-CAT, drone, bubble.",
"First described in 1929, it became popular in the UK from about 2007 as a legal high and was banned there in 2010.","None.","Popular in clubs as an MDMA-like stimulant; injected in some chemsex scenes.","US Schedule I (2012); UN 1971 Schedule II (2015).",
["Mephedrone feels like a mix of MDMA and cocaine: euphoria, empathy, talkativeness and energy, but short-lived, with a strong urge to redose.","Users report sweating, a pounding heart, jaw clenching and cold or bluish fingers from narrowed blood vessels.","Heavy use causes paranoia and insomnia, and injecting causes abscesses and infections."],
["Snorted, effects start within 10 to 20 minutes.","A fast rise of euphoria.","Peak within 30 to 60 minutes.","Effects last 1 to 2 hours.","Comedown with craving.","Anxiety and insomnia.","Low mood for a day or two."],
{"intranasal":t("10-20 min","15-30 min","30-60 min","1-2 h","2-4 h","4-12 h","next day"),"oral":t("15-45 min","30-60 min","1-2 h","2-3 h","3-5 h","hours","next day")},{"intranasal":"Snorted.","oral":"Swallowed."},
"Overdose risks include overheating, seizures, heart problems and psychosis.","White or off-white powder or crystals, sometimes with a fishy smell."+CW,"Mephedrone releases dopamine and serotonin.",src=["EMCDDA: Risk assessment report on mephedrone (2010)"])

X("mdpv","MDPV","cath","Potent synthetic cathinone; dopamine and noradrenaline reuptake inhibitor.","Bath salts, monkey dust, cloud nine, ivory wave.",
"First patented by Boehringer Ingelheim in 1969; sold as 'bath salts' from about 2009 to 2011, causing a wave of US emergency visits.","None.","Sold as bath salts or plant food; linked to violent agitation and psychosis.","US Schedule I (emergency 2011, permanent 2013); UN 1971 Schedule II (2015).",
["MDPV blocks dopamine reuptake far more strongly than cocaine, causing intense stimulation, euphoria and compulsive redosing.","High levels cause terror, paranoia, hallucinations and violent agitation; people may sweat heavily and overheat.","Effects can last many hours and cause muscle breakdown, kidney failure and death."],
["Snorted, effects start within minutes.","A steep rise in energy.","Peak within 1 to 2 hours.","Effects last 3 to 4 hours, but long binges are common.","A hard crash.","Days of insomnia and paranoia.","Lasting depression and craving."],
{"intranasal":t("5-15 min","15-30 min","1-2 h","2-4 h","4-8 h","1-3 days","days")},{"intranasal":"Snorted."},"MDPV poisoning causes overheating, agitation, heart failure and kidney failure.","White or tan powder."+CW,"MDPV strongly blocks dopamine reuptake.",src=["CDC: Emergency department visits after use of 'bath salts', Michigan 2010-2011 (MMWR)"])

X("alpha-pvp","Alpha-PVP (flakka)","cath","Synthetic cathinone closely related to MDPV.","Flakka, gravel.",
"Appeared around 2012 and caused a public health crisis in South Florida in 2015.","None.","Cheap, extremely potent, and linked to bizarre, violent behavior.","US Schedule I (2014); UN 1971 Schedule II (2015).",
["Alpha-PVP causes a fast, intense high with racing thoughts and a powerful urge to redose.","Excited delirium is common at higher levels: paranoia, agitation, very high temperature and extreme strength reported by paramedics.","Deaths from overheating, heart failure and kidney failure have been reported."],
["Smoked or snorted, effects begin within minutes.","Rapid stimulation.","Peak within an hour.","Effects last a few hours.","A hard crash with paranoia.","Insomnia.","Days of low mood."],
{"inhaled-smoked":t("seconds-minutes","minutes","15-60 min","1-3 h","3-6 h","1-2 days","days"),"intranasal":t("minutes","10-20 min","30-60 min","1-3 h","3-6 h","1-2 days","days")},{"inhaled-smoked":"Smoked or vaped.","intranasal":"Snorted."},
"Flakka poisoning causes overheating, excited delirium and death.","White or pink crystals ('gravel')."+CW,"A potent dopamine reuptake blocker.",src=["NIDA: Flakka (alpha-PVP) emerging trends"])

X("methylone","Methylone and eutylone","cath","Synthetic cathinones with MDMA-like effects.","Methylone (bk-MDMA, M1), eutylone (bk-EBDB); often sold as 'molly'.",
"Methylone was patented as an antidepressant in 1996 and sold in the Netherlands as 'Explosion' around 2004. Eutylone replaced it around 2019 and was the most common cathinone in US 'molly' samples in 2020.","None; methylone has been studied for PTSD.","Commonly sold as MDMA at festivals.","Methylone is US Schedule I (2013) and UN Schedule II; eutylone is US Schedule I and UN Schedule II (2022).",
["Methylone feels like a weaker, shorter MDMA: euphoria, empathy and energy, with more stimulation and less warmth.","Eutylone is more stimulating and longer, causing insomnia, anxiety and redosing.","People expecting MDMA may take more when effects feel different, raising risks of overheating and seizures."],
["Swallowed, effects begin within 30 to 60 minutes.","A rise of energy and euphoria.","Peak around 1 to 2 hours.","Effects last 3 to 4 hours (eutylone longer).","Comedown with anxiety.","Insomnia.","Low mood the next day."],
{"oral":t("30-60 min","about 1 h","1-2 h","2-4 h","4-6 h","6-24 h","next day")},{"oral":"Capsules or crystals."},"Overheating, seizures and low blood sodium have caused deaths.","Off-white crystals or capsules sold as 'molly'."+CW,"These release serotonin and dopamine.",src=["DEA: Eutylone scheduling actions","NPS Discovery (Center for Forensic Science Research and Education) reports"])

X("other-cathinones","Other synthetic cathinones (N-ethylpentylone, 3-MMC, 4-MEC, hexen and others)","cath","A large family of designer stimulants.","N-ethylpentylone (ephylone), 3-MMC, 4-MEC, pentedrone, N-ethylhexedrone (hexen), alpha-PHP.",
"New cathinones have appeared every year since about 2010 to replace banned ones; European monitoring has identified more than 150.","None.","Sold as bath salts, 'molly', or by chemical name.","Many are Schedule I in the US and UN-controlled; others fall under analog laws.",
["Each cathinone feels somewhat different, from MDMA-like to cocaine-like, but all cause stimulation, a fast heart rate and compulsive redosing.","N-ethylpentylone caused clusters of deaths and hospitalizations in the US and New Zealand around 2017 and 2018.","Because products are mislabeled, users rarely know which one they have."],
["Effects begin within minutes (snorted) to an hour (swallowed).","Stimulation rises.","Peak within 1 to 2 hours.","Effects last a few hours; binges are common.","Hard crash.","Insomnia and paranoia.","Days of low mood."],
{"intranasal":t("5-20 min","15-30 min","30-90 min","1-3 h","3-6 h","1-2 days","days"),"oral":t("30-60 min","about 1 h","1-2 h","2-4 h","4-8 h","1-2 days","days")},{"intranasal":"Snorted.","oral":"Swallowed."},"Overheating, seizures, heart failure and psychosis.","Powders and crystals of varying color."+CW,"Varying dopamine, noradrenaline and serotonin effects.",src=["EMCDDA/EUDA: European Drug Report 2024"])
